-- Migration 19: EVL-02 experiments — the ARM table, and the two columns
-- migration 18 deliberately left for this item.
--
-- Spec: `specs/EVL-02-experiments-and-side-by-side-diff.md` §2.2 (corrected
-- 2026-08-23). Migration 18's own header names this file and says why:
-- `experiments` was created EMPTY-ish and OWNED BY ITEM 9, so item 9 starts
-- from an ALTER rather than from a second, conflicting CREATE.
--
--
-- ── DO NOT EDIT THIS FILE AFTER IT HAS BEEN APPLIED ─────────────────────────
--
-- Same rule, same reason as 18: ClickHouse migrations here are applied PER FILE
-- BY HAND with no journal table, so nothing would notice a file changing after
-- it ran. An edited applied file is silent prod drift in the direction nobody
-- checks. A further change goes in `20_...`.
--
--
-- ── WHAT THIS FILE DOES *NOT* DO, AND WHY IT IS SHORTER THAN THE SPEC ───────
--
-- `EVL-02` §2.2's ORIGINAL DDL block proposed `CREATE TABLE experiments`,
-- `experiment_arms` AND `eval_run_items`. Two of those three ALREADY EXIST in
-- migration 18, applied to prod, with different column spellings and a
-- different engine. Building to the original text would have produced a second
-- definition of two live tables. §17: the code wins, the spec is the defect —
-- and the spec was corrected in the same change rather than routed around.
--
-- So: NO `RENAME COLUMN` here either. §2.2's correction block settles the two
-- divergences IN FAVOUR OF THE SHIPPED TABLE — `experiments.snapshot_id` (not
-- `dataset_snapshot_id`) and `experiments.created_by` (not `created_by_user_id`)
-- — because both already match the sibling `datasets` table, and matching the
-- table beside it is worth more than matching a document.
--
--
-- ── THE THREE ALTER-IMMUTABLE PROPERTIES, ARGUED BEFORE THIS FILE LANDS ─────
--
-- Migration 18's header asks, in bold, for the ENGINE / ORDER BY / PARTITION BY
-- argument to be had BEFORE a file is applied, and records that nobody had it
-- for 18 (founder ruling R107 — that is the process finding, and it is worth
-- more than the schema). So, for `experiment_arms`, in advance:
--
--   ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at). An arm row is MUTABLE: it is
--     written when the experiment is created (before its `eval_run_id` exists,
--     which is why that column is Nullable) and rewritten when the arm's run
--     starts and again when it finishes. `updated_at` is NOT NULL, so the
--     VERSIONED form is legal — and it keeps the row with the highest
--     `updated_at` rather than "whichever landed last", which is not the same
--     thing when a status write races.
--
--   ORDER BY (tenant_id, experiment_id, arm_id). Every read is
--     `WHERE tenant_id = ? AND experiment_id = ?` — the detail page, the compare
--     page and the arm resolver all take that shape, and nothing here is ever
--     read by `arm_id` alone. The prefix therefore prunes every query we have.
--     It is also a natural dedup key, which is what makes the Replacing engine
--     free rather than a compromise.
--
--   PARTITION BY tuple(). Two reasons, both migration 18's, both still true:
--     (a) no read filters a time range, so a monthly partition prunes nothing
--         while multiplying the part count; and
--     (b) on a ReplacingMergeTree, a partition key derived from a column the
--         update path rewrites is the B-243 shape — parts merge only WITHIN a
--         partition, so two versions of one row in different partitions can
--         never collapse.
--
-- `eval_run_items` KEEPS `PARTITION BY toYYYYMM(started_at)` from 18. It is the
-- one unbounded, append-only table here and `started_at` never moves.
--
--
-- ── DateTime64(3) MEANS MILLIS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Same trap as 18, restated because it shipped twice: `clickhouse-rs` maps a
-- plain `i64` onto the column's RAW TICKS with NO unit conversion, so a
-- `timestamp_micros()` at an insert site is accepted, never errors, and reads
-- back as `2299-12-31`. Use `datetime64_millis_now()`
-- (`crates/gateway/src/clickhouse_query.rs`) at EVERY insert site.
--
--
-- ── NO ROW POLICIES, DELIBERATELY ───────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Migration 18 §"NO ROW POLICIES" applies verbatim: prod has
-- `count() FROM system.row_policies = 0` instance-wide, and the gateway never
-- sets the `X-Tenant-Id` header they would test. Declaring one would add a
-- control that has never functioned, which reads as a control and is not one
-- (`CLAUDE.md` §1). Isolation is the explicit `WHERE tenant_id = ?` bind,
-- enforced by `scripts/ci/check-tenant-isolation.py`, which has no exemption
-- mechanism.


-- ── experiments: the two columns migration 18 left to this item ─────────────
--
-- `item_count` is the SNAPSHOT's frozen item count, copied at creation. It is a
-- HISTORICAL FACT about an immutable set, not a running total, so it cannot
-- drift — the same distinction migration 18 draws for
-- `dataset_snapshots.item_count` while refusing a stored counter on `datasets`.
-- It is what makes `41 / 50` renderable: the numerator is a count of written
-- item rows and the denominator MUST come from the frozen set, or a run that
-- stopped early would silently render as complete.
ALTER TABLE experiments ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS item_count UInt32 DEFAULT 0;

-- Free text the creator attaches to the experiment. Never interpreted.
ALTER TABLE experiments ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS notes String DEFAULT '';


-- ── experiment_arms: one row per arm ────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- THE ARM *IS* AN `eval_runs` ROW. `eval_runs` is extended by ZERO columns and
-- this table carries the experiment linkage instead. That is deliberate: an arm
-- inherits `eval_runs`' lifecycle, its four-string status vocabulary and — the
-- part that matters — `reconcile_stale_runs`
-- (`crates/gateway/src/prompt_eval.rs`), so a gateway killed mid-arm already has
-- crash recovery with nothing new written for it.
--
-- `eval_run_id` IS Nullable AND NULL MEANS "THIS ARM HAS NOT STARTED YET",
-- NEVER "the run is gone". Arms run SEQUENTIALLY (founder ruling R82), so at
-- most one arm of an experiment is running and the arms behind it genuinely
-- have no run id yet. The all-zero UUID was rejected for this: it is a legal
-- value that reads as an id, and every consumer would have to remember to test
-- for it — the same argument `eval_run_items.cost_usd` makes for Nullable over
-- a sentinel zero.
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS experiment_arms (
    tenant_id          String,                   -- validated claim ONLY, never a body
    experiment_id      UUID,
    arm_id             UUID,
    -- The user's label for the arm. Empty means unlabelled and the surface
    -- renders the ordinal — it does NOT invent a name. Plain String, not
    -- LowCardinality, for migration 18's reason: a dictionary per part is worth
    -- it for a four-value status vocabulary and a pure cost for free text.
    arm_label          String DEFAULT '',
    ordinal            UInt8,                    -- 0-based. FIXES the column order
    eval_run_id        Nullable(UUID),           -- NULL = not started. NEVER all-zero
    prompt_version_id  UUID,
    model              String,
    -- The arm's own terminal state, mirrored from its `eval_runs` row so the
    -- list page renders without a join per arm. `pending` is the fifth string
    -- and it exists ONLY here: it is the state of an arm whose run has not been
    -- created, which `eval_runs` cannot represent because it has no row yet.
    -- It is NOT written into `eval_runs` and cannot reach
    -- `ClickHouseEvalGate::status`, whose vocabulary is exactly four.
    status             LowCardinality(String),   -- pending | running | passed | failed | errored
    created_at         DateTime64(3, 'UTC'),     -- MILLIS. datetime64_millis_now()
    updated_at         DateTime64(3, 'UTC')      -- MILLIS. the version column
)
ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at)
ORDER BY (tenant_id, experiment_id, arm_id)
PARTITION BY tuple();
